# tools/cr/crypto_monocypher_elligator_parity_witness.rish — Monocypher-source parity for Elligator 2 over Curve25519 (crypto_elligator_map /
# crypto_elligator_rev), the map that hides an X25519 public key as a uniformly random 32-byte string and reads it back. Our authored
# crypto/elligator.rye and the vendored Monocypher's own C (vendor/monocypher, CC0/BSD-dual), each compiled fresh and asked for the same
# thirty-two direct and inverse answers, diffed byte-for-byte. The SEVENTEENTH rung of the real Monocypher-source parity pattern (BLAKE2b ·
# X25519 · Ed25519 · IETF AEAD · Argon2 · XChaCha20-Poly1305 flagship · Edwards↔Montgomery · Poly1305 · ChaCha20 · SHA-512 · HMAC-SHA-512 ·
# HChaCha20 · variable BLAKE2b · keyed BLAKE2b · constant-time verify · HKDF-SHA-512 came before). Season G · Cryptography.
#
# Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season G thread (Cryptography · Rye-native, Monocypher-parity, audit-ready).
# Design read: active-designing/date/20260815/20260815-175524_rye-first-crypto-parity-and-the-decision-wave.md — Season G's crux names "the parity witness
# proving Rye output equals Monocypher output byte-for-byte." This rung is the first for a primitive Zig's std.crypto does not ship at all, so
# unlike the hash and cipher rungs there is no independent std.crypto oracle beside the parity — the byte-for-byte agreement with the actual
# vendored library IS the external anchor, standing beside the round-trip identity and Monocypher's own published vectors proven in the module's
# own witness (tools/cr/crypto_elligator_witness.rish).
# Kin: crypto/elligator.rye (our authored primitive, GREEN against Monocypher's own elligator_dir/elligator_inv vectors and the round-trip
# identity) · tools/rye/monocypher_elligator_parity.rye (our printing harness) · tools/fixtures/monocypher_elligator_harness.c (Monocypher's
# printing harness) · crypto/fe25519.rye (the field both stand on). Monocypher stays the parity TARGET, vendored and unmodified, never a copied
# line, studied clean-room (.claude/rules/gratitude-licenses.md).
#
# The thirty-two lines: sixteen DIRECT answers (a representative hidden[j] = (i*7 + j*3 + 1) mod 256 to its 64-hex u-coordinate) then sixteen
# INVERSE answers (a point pubkey[j] = (i*11 + j*5 + 2) mod 256 under tweak (i*13 + 7) mod 256, printing the 64-hex representative on success or
# "fail" when the point is not representable). Roughly half the points are representable, so the run mixes success and failure — a regression
# that inverted representability or drifted a branch goes RED. The witness additionally asserts at least one "fail" and at least one hex line
# appear, so a harness that silently printed one outcome for every case cannot pass. Purely local — no key, no network, no funds, no device;
# constant-time timing-safety stays the Season's named horizon (this proves the MAP's byte-for-byte correctness, not a timing claim).
#
# Run from the repository root:
#   rishi/bin/rishi run tools/cr/crypto_monocypher_elligator_parity_witness.rish

say "crypto-monocypher-elligator-parity: Language EN — Monocypher-source parity for Elligator 2 over Curve25519 (crypto_elligator_map / crypto_elligator_rev), the map that hides an X25519 public key as a uniformly random 32-byte string. This seventeenth rung proves our authored crypto/elligator.rye against the ACTUAL vendored Monocypher C over sixteen direct and sixteen inverse answers, byte-for-byte."
say "crypto-monocypher-elligator-parity: Style Radiant — like against like. Our authored primitive and the unmodified vendored Monocypher each compile fresh and each print, in order, sixteen direct representative→u-coordinate lines then sixteen inverse point+tweak→representative-or-fail lines over the same deterministic inputs. Two independent implementations, one set of cases; the parity is that all thirty-two lines agree exactly — the failures fail together, the representatives match to the byte."
say "crypto-monocypher-elligator-parity: Lens TAME — Zig ships no Elligator, so the oracle here is the second real implementation (Monocypher) agreeing, beside the round-trip identity and Monocypher's own vectors proven in the module's own witness. The witness diffs the thirty-two Monocypher lines against the thirty-two Rye lines byte-for-byte AND asserts both a fail and a hex line appear, so a harness printing one outcome for all cannot pass. Monocypher is linked, never copied. Purely local — no key, no network, no funds, no device; constant-time timing-safety stays the named horizon."

let zig = "vendor/zig-toolchain/zig"
let cbin = "crypto/bin/monocypher_elligator"
let cout = "crypto/bin/monocypher_elligator.out"
let rout = "crypto/bin/rye_elligator.out"

# 1. Build the Monocypher C harness, linking the unmodified vendored source with zig cc.
let cbuild = run ["sh" "-c" "mkdir -p crypto/bin && ${zig} cc -O2 -I vendor/monocypher/src tools/fixtures/monocypher_elligator_harness.c vendor/monocypher/src/monocypher.c -o ${cbin}"]
assert cbuild.ok else "crypto-monocypher-elligator-parity: Monocypher C harness build failed — ${cbuild.err}"

# 2. Run the Monocypher C harness — thirty-two lines.
let crun = run ["sh" "-c" "${cbin} > ${cout} 2>&1"]
assert crun.ok else "crypto-monocypher-elligator-parity: Monocypher C harness exited non-zero — see ${cout}"

# 3. Run our authored Rye harness — thirty-two lines over the identical inputs.
let rrun = run ["sh" "-c" "env RYE_ZIG=${zig} rye/bin/rye run tools/rye/monocypher_elligator_parity.rye > ${rout} 2>&1"]
assert rrun.ok else "crypto-monocypher-elligator-parity: Rye harness exited non-zero — see ${rout}"

# 4. Byte-for-byte parity: the two thirty-two-line outputs must be identical.
let diff = run ["sh" "-c" "diff ${cout} ${rout} > /dev/null && echo same"]
assert (trim diff.out) == "same" else "crypto-monocypher-elligator-parity: Monocypher and Rye answers disagree — Monocypher:\n$(cat ${cout})\nRye:\n$(cat ${rout})"

# 5. Anchor the mix: the run must exercise BOTH outcomes of the inverse map, so a harness that printed one outcome for every case cannot pass.
let n_fail = run ["sh" "-c" "grep -c '^fail$' ${cout} || true"]
assert (trim n_fail.out) != "0" else "crypto-monocypher-elligator-parity: no unrepresentable point in the run — the inverse-map failure path was never exercised"
let n_hex = run ["sh" "-c" "grep -cE '^[0-9a-f]{64}$' ${cout} || true"]
assert (trim n_hex.out) != "0" else "crypto-monocypher-elligator-parity: no representable answer in the run — the success path was never exercised"

let shown = run ["sh" "-c" "cat ${cout}"]
say "crypto-monocypher-elligator-parity: the thirty-two answers, agreed by both implementations:\n${shown.out}"

say "GREEN crypto-monocypher-elligator-parity: our authored crypto/elligator.rye and the vendored Monocypher's own C, each compiled fresh, produce byte-for-byte identical Elligator 2 answers (crypto_elligator_map / crypto_elligator_rev) over sixteen direct representative→u-coordinate cases and sixteen inverse point+tweak→representative-or-fail cases, the failures failing together and the representatives matching to the byte. The SEVENTEENTH real Monocypher-SOURCE parity rung, and the first for a primitive Zig's std.crypto does not ship — so the second real implementation agreeing is the external anchor beside the round-trip identity and Monocypher's own vectors. Monocypher stays vendored and unmodified, linked never copied. Purely local, no key, no network, no funds, no device; constant-time timing-safety stays the named horizon, the inverse map's one representability test deliberately variable-time by design."
